on_margin":0.4,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'Because read_reserved_section merges the user-file and project-file sub-tables shallowly, this module deep-merges them (via _deep_merge) first, so a user-file default.status_map survives when a project file declares only projects.<KEY>.'","config.py:610-619 — for each of the user then project file, `sub = table.get(name)` then `merged.update(sub)`: a top-level dict.update, so the second file's `default` (or `projects`) VALUE wholesale-replaces the first file's same key.","The plan's own worked example only covers the DISJOINT case (user declares default, project declares only projects.<KEY>). It does not address both files declaring `default` (or both declaring `projects`), where the user-file nested content is lost at the reserved-section layer before _deep_merge runs."],"finding":"The cross-file deep-merge compensation is defeated for colliding top-level keys because read_reserved_section performs a shallow `merged.update(sub)` that replaces the whole colliding key before the module can deep-merge.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f4f5fb34a6bb1ef5f","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 1 (cross-file deep-merge rationale) and Testing bullet 'a user-file default.status_map survives when the project file declares only projects.REB'","norm_id":"n854acd32ec0632f6","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["User pyproject sets [tool.rebar.mapping].default.status_map plus default.type_map; project pyproject also sets [tool.rebar.mapping].default.status_map — read_reserved_section returns only the project file's `default` table, silently dropping the user's default.type_map, so the module's _deep_merge never sees it.","Both files declare projects.REB with different axis sub-tables — the user file's projects.REB is discarded wholesale."],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_co